/* Lesson quizzes — batch 6 */
window.LQ=window.LQ||{};
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p12m1":[
 {id:"p12m1q1", q:"Last month Hermes Agent kept fumbling your backup procedure; this month it gets it right first time. You open ~/.hermes/ to see what changed. What do you expect to find?", options:["A newer, retrained copy of the model weights in the models folder","A new or edited SKILL.md under skills/ plus updated memory notes","A fine-tuning log showing the model trained on last month's sessions","Nothing on disk, because the improvement lives in the model's hidden state"], answer:1, why:"Self-improving in Hermes means it writes and edits skill and memory files and searches past sessions. The weights never change, so a retrained model or a fine-tuning log is the tempting but wrong idea; the improvement sits in files you can read and delete."},
 {id:"p12m1q2", q:"You ask: 'Count the lines in every .log file under /var/log/myapp.' The model replies with a terminal tool call: wc -l /var/log/myapp/*.log. Which step happens in the harness, not in the model?", options:["Deciding that wc -l is the right command for this task","Writing the final sentence that reports the line counts","Choosing the terminal tool instead of answering from memory","Executing wc -l and feeding its output back as an observation"], answer:3, why:"The model only emits a structured request to call a tool; the harness is ordinary code that runs the command (after approval if risky) and returns the output. Picking the command and writing the reply are the model's think steps, not the act step."},
 {id:"p12m1q3", q:"A two-person dental office wants Hermes to summarize patient intake forms and, now and then, help debug their booking website. Which setup best fits the lesson's advice?", options:["A local model for patient forms; a cloud frontier model only for the website code","A cloud frontier model for everything, since it beats local models on reasoning","A local model for everything, since local always beats cloud on every task","A hosted model for the forms, since the agent and memory stay local anyway"], answer:0, why:"Experts mix: a local model keeps patient data on the machine, while a frontier cloud model handles hard but non-sensitive work. The hosted-for-forms option tempts, but with a hosted provider the prompt, including the form text, is processed in the cloud."},
 {id:"p12m1q4", q:"Hermes is configured with OpenRouter as its provider. You ask it to read ~/contracts/acme.txt and summarize it. Which statement about where the data goes is accurate?", options:["Nothing leaves your machine, because the agent and its tools run locally","The whole ~/contracts folder is uploaded so the provider can index it","The file's text travels to the provider in the prompt; the read ran locally","Only the filename leaves; the provider's model opens the file remotely"], answer:2, why:"With a hosted provider the agent, tools and memory stay local, so the file is read on your machine, but its contents go into the prompt and are processed in the cloud. 'Nothing leaves' is the tempting error; that holds only with a local model."},
 {id:"p12m1q5", q:"Hermes pauses for approval before running rm -rf ~/build/*. A friend says to start it with --yolo on your main laptop so it stops asking. What is the sound response?", options:["Use --yolo, but run Hermes as root so permission errors stop interrupting","Keep approvals on here and reserve --yolo for a throwaway container or VM","Use --yolo, because the harness verifies every command before running it","Use --yolo, since rm -rf inside your home directory cannot harm the system"], answer:1, why:"The approval step is what stands between a hallucinated destructive command and your files; --yolo removes it, so mastery means keeping it for disposable sandboxes. Running as root is the opposite of the discipline, and your home directory holds the files you care about most."}
],
"p12m2":[
 {id:"p12m2q1", q:"On Windows 11 you set up Ubuntu under WSL2 and also installed NVIDIA's Linux driver inside Ubuntu. Now nvidia-smi inside WSL errors. What does the official guide say the setup should be?", options:["Install the CUDA Linux driver inside WSL and remove the Windows driver","Install only the Windows NVIDIA driver; WSL passes the GPU through","Enable mirrored networking so WSL can reach the GPU over the network","Enable systemd in /etc/wsl.conf so the driver loads as a service"], answer:1, why:"For an NVIDIA GPU under WSL2 you install only the Windows driver, and nvidia-smi inside WSL confirms passthrough. A Linux driver inside the distro works against that design. Mirrored networking and systemd solve other problems, not GPU access."},
 {id:"p12m2q2", q:"Hermes runs inside WSL2; LM Studio runs on the Windows side, listening on port 1234. From WSL, connecting to localhost:1234 fails. Which fix does the lesson give?", options:["Move LM Studio's model folder under /mnt/c so both sides can share a single copy of it","Install the NVIDIA Linux driver inside WSL so it can reach the server","Run hermes update --set-channel main to pick up newer networking code","Set mirrored networking in .wslconfig, or bind to 0.0.0.0 and use the host IP"], answer:3, why:"A server on the Windows side is not WSL's localhost by default. Mirrored networking (Windows 11 22H2+) makes it so; otherwise bind the server to all interfaces and use the host's IP. Channel switches and drivers do not change WSL's network path."},
 {id:"p12m2q3", q:"You want the Hermes Telegram gateway to keep running inside WSL2 after you close the terminal window. Which approach matches the lesson's advice for WSL specifically?", options:["Run it in a tmux session, or start it from a Windows Task Scheduler job","Register it with hermes gateway install as a systemd unit inside WSL","Run it with --yolo so it never waits on an approval and stalls","Move it into the Docker image and run hermes update to keep it alive"], answer:0, why:"Systemd services are unreliable under WSL, so the guide says to run the gateway in tmux or through a Windows Task Scheduler job. A systemd unit is the natural choice on real Linux, which is why it tempts, but under WSL it may not stay up."},
 {id:"p12m2q4", q:"You run Hermes from the official Docker image, with your data mounted at /opt/data/. A new release is out. What is the correct way to update?", options:["Run hermes update --backup inside the container, then restart it","Run the install.sh one-liner inside the container to replace the code","Pull the new image and recreate the container with the same data mount","Run hermes update --set-channel stable so the container fetches it"], answer:2, why:"hermes update is not supported inside Docker; you update by pulling a new image. Because your data lives on the /opt/data mount rather than in the image, recreating the container keeps memory, skills and sessions. Running hermes update inside is the tempting, unsupported path."},
 {id:"p12m2q5", q:"Your Hermes code is broken after experiments and you want a clean reinstall, but you must keep your memory, skills, sessions and keys. What do you do before reinstalling?", options:["Run hermes uninstall --full, since it performs a complete clean removal","Run hermes uninstall --data, since it targets only the program's files","Run rm -rf ~/.hermes/ and then rerun the installer from scratch","Back up ~/.hermes/, preview with --dry-run, then run plain hermes uninstall"], answer:3, why:"Plain hermes uninstall removes the program; --full also deletes the data directory and --data deletes only data, the opposite of what you want. ~/.hermes/ holds memory, skills, sessions and keys (and the code), so back it up and preview with --dry-run first."}
],
"p12m3":[
 {id:"p12m3q1", q:"You taught Hermes a deploy-check skill while working in the CLI. That evening you message your Telegram bot: 'run deploy-check.' What happens, and why?", options:["It fails; the bot is a separate program and must be taught the skill again","It works; the gateway and the CLI are entry points into the same AIAgent core","It works only after you export the CLI session as a trajectory to the bot","It fails; skills are stored per session, so a new chat cannot see them"], answer:1, why:"Every surface (CLI, gateway, ACP, API server, cron) feeds one AIAgent class, and skills live in ~/.hermes/skills/, so they are shared. Treating the CLI and the bot as different programs is the classic misconception; trajectories are training data, not a sync mechanism."},
 {id:"p12m3q2", q:"Halfway through a long session on Anthropic you switch with /model to a different provider. The next reply is slower and bills many more input tokens. What best explains it?", options:["The context compressor always runs whenever the model is changed","Hermes reloads every skill from disk and recompiles the tool list","The cached stable prefix is lost, so the full prompt is processed again","The new model must be fine-tuned on your memory files before replying"], answer:2, why:"The prompt's stable tier exists so a provider can reuse an unchanged prefix from its cache; a new model or provider has no cache for it, so the whole prompt is processed and billed fresh. Compression is triggered by context size, not by a model switch."},
 {id:"p12m3q3", q:"Your model has a 128K-token context window and compression uses its default trigger. The session holds 40 turns when it fires. Roughly when does it fire, and which turns get summarized?", options:["Near 64K tokens; turns 4 through 20 are summarized","Near 128K tokens; turns 4 through 20 are summarized","Near 64K tokens; turns 1 through 20 are summarized","Near 64K tokens; turns 21 through 37 are summarized"], answer:0, why:"The default trigger is half the context window, about 64K here. The compressor protects the first three and the last twenty turns, so with 40 turns it summarizes turns 4 to 20. Waiting for the full 128K is the tempting error; by then there is no room left."},
 {id:"p12m3q4", q:"You serve a model with your own llama-server on a home box and add it to Hermes. Of Hermes' three underlying API modes, which one will it use to talk to that server?", options:["Anthropic's messages format, since Hermes prompts are tuned for it","The OpenAI responses mode, because it is the newest of the three","ACP over stdio JSON-RPC, the protocol Hermes uses for integrations","OpenAI-style chat completions, the format local servers speak"], answer:3, why:"Local servers speak OpenAI-style chat completions, which is why 'OpenAI-compatible' appears everywhere in local AI. The responses and messages modes serve specific hosted providers, and ACP connects editors such as VS Code and Zed, not model servers."},
 {id:"p12m3q5", q:"In the Telegram bot, Hermes replies 'I'll search the web for that' but no search ever runs; in the CLI the same request works. Where is the fix?", options:["Switch to a larger model, because web search is built into the weights","Enable the web toolset for the messaging surface with hermes tools","Add the search results to MEMORY.md so the bot can read them later","Clear state.db, because a corrupted session store blocks tool calls"], answer:1, why:"Tools are harness code grouped into toolsets that are enabled per surface with hermes tools. The model only emits a request to call one; if the toolset is off for that surface, nothing runs. A bigger model cannot help, because tools are not inside the model."}
],
"p12m4":[
 {id:"p12m4q1", q:"Your GPU has 12 GB of VRAM, about 11 GB usable after the display and driver. Which option would the recommender most plausibly show in green with the required 64K context?", options:["A 20B model at 5 bits per weight, about 12.5 GB of weights","A 14B model at 8 bits per weight, about 14 GB of weights","A 14B model at 4.5 bits per weight, about 7.9 GB of weights","An 8B model at 3 bits per weight, about 3 GB, the smallest footprint"], answer:2, why:"Weights are about parameters × bits ÷ 8: 14 × 4.5 ÷ 8 ≈ 7.9 GB, leaving roughly 3 GB for the KV cache. The 20B and 8-bit options exceed the budget, and the recommender never offers below 4-bit, so the tiny 3-bit build is not offered despite its size."},
 {id:"p12m4q2", q:"You run vllm serve with --max-model-len 65536 and point Hermes at it. It chats normally but never runs a single tool, even for 'list my files.' What is missing?", options:["--enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser hermes on the vLLM command","A longer context, such as --max-model-len 131072, to fit the tool list","--jinja -fa -ngl 99 on the vLLM command, which turns on tool support","OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH=64000 set in the environment before starting vLLM"], answer:0, why:"Every server has a flag that turns tool calling on; for vLLM it is --enable-auto-tool-choice with a tool-call parser. Without it the model chats but never acts. --jinja belongs to llama-server, OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH to Ollama, and 65,536 already meets the 64K minimum."},
 {id:"p12m4q3", q:"Box A has a CPU with 128 GB of system RAM; box B has a GPU with 24 GB of fast VRAM. Both run the same 20 GB quantized model. Which generates tokens faster, and why?", options:["Box A, because more total memory always means faster generation","About the same, because the model and quantization are identical","Box A, because the CPU can spread the work across all of its cores and the larger pool","Box B, because tokens per second follow memory bandwidth, and VRAM is faster"], answer:3, why:"Generation speed scales with memory bandwidth more than with capacity or compute. The model fits in B's VRAM, so B wins. 'More RAM means faster' is the misconception; capacity only lets the model load, and weights served from system RAM crawl."},
 {id:"p12m4q4", q:"A model runs well at 8K context with about 2 GB of VRAM to spare. You raise the context to 64K and it slows sharply as memory spills to system RAM. What explains it?", options:["Longer contexts make the runtime reload the weights at a higher bit depth","The KV cache grows linearly with context, so 8× the context needs far more memory","A 64K context turns on tool calling, which loads a second full copy of the model weights","The idle timer unloaded the model and reloaded it into system RAM instead"], answer:1, why:"The KV cache holds attention state for every token in the context and grows linearly with its length; at 64K it can need several extra gigabytes, overflowing the 2 GB headroom. Weight size depends on parameters and bits per weight, not on context length."},
 {id:"p12m4q5", q:"You use the managed llama.cpp runtime. After a 30-minute lunch break, your first question takes much longer before any text appears; later ones are fast again. What happened?", options:["The download lock file expired, so the weights were downloaded again","The backend fell back from CUDA to CPU while the machine sat idle","The model was unloaded after 15 idle minutes and had to be reloaded","The context compressor summarized the whole session during the break"], answer:2, why:"The managed runtime unloads idle models after fifteen minutes to free memory, so the first request after a break pays the load time. Downloads are pinned by URL and SHA-256 and are not repeated, and compression is triggered by context size, not idle time."}
],
"p12m5":[
 {id:"p12m5q1", q:"You run hermes config set OPENROUTER_API_KEY sk-or-123 and then hermes config set model.context_length 65536. Where does each value end up?", options:["Both in config.yaml, which holds every Hermes setting","Both in .env, since anything set from the CLI is treated as secret","The key in config.yaml and the context length in .env","The key in .env and the context length in config.yaml"], answer:3, why:"hermes config set routes UPPER_SNAKE names to .env and dotted keys to config.yaml. That keeps secrets in .env (mode 600) and non-secret settings in config.yaml. An API key in config.yaml is exactly what the security checklist forbids."},
 {id:"p12m5q2", q:"A nightly script runs hermes -z 'rotate the app logs' and checks the exit code. Last night the code was 2. What does that tell you to look at first?", options:["The script's invocation, since 2 is a usage error such as a bad flag","The task itself, since 2 means the agent tried the job and failed","Nothing, since 2 means the run was interrupted by a signal","The provider, since 2 means the model returned no answer at all"], answer:0, why:"Headless mode exits 0 on success, 1 on failure, 2 on a usage error and 130 when interrupted. A 2 points at how the script called Hermes, not at the task. Reading it as a task failure (that is code 1) sends you debugging the wrong thing."},
 {id:"p12m5q3", q:"At 9:00 you send a friend a pairing code for your Discord bot. They try it at 11:15 and it is rejected. What is the right next step?", options:["Post a fresh code in the server's public channel so they can find it","Generate a new code and send it privately, treating it like a password","Turn off default deny for an hour so the friend can connect","Restart the gateway, which revalidates every code issued earlier"], answer:1, why:"Pairing codes expire after an hour, so a 9:00 code is dead by 11:15. Issue a fresh one and send it privately, because a pairing code grants access the way a password does. Posting it publicly or disabling default deny opens the bot to anyone."},
 {id:"p12m5q4", q:"You created ~/.hermes/skills/backup-check/ containing only backup.sh. Hermes never offers /backup-check and never uses it. What is the most likely fix?", options:["Make backup.sh executable, because skills appear once the script can run","Move the folder to ~/.hermes/plugins/, where custom commands are loaded","Add a SKILL.md whose front matter names the skill and when to use it","Add the script's path to MEMORY.md so the agent remembers it exists"], answer:2, why:"A skill is a folder with a SKILL.md (front matter naming it and saying when to use it, then the procedure) plus any scripts. Without SKILL.md there is no skill to load or to show as a slash command. Plugins are a different mechanism, and memory is not a skill registry."},
 {id:"p12m5q5", q:"After fixing a tricky deploy, Hermes wrote itself a new skill whose steps pipe a script downloaded from a URL straight into bash. What does the lesson's checklist say to do?", options:["Nothing, since agent-written skills are sandboxed and cannot run commands","Delete every agent-written skill, since only bundled skills are safe","Run it once with --yolo to confirm it works, then read it afterwards","Review it like a pull request before relying on it, and fix or drop that step"], answer:3, why:"Agent-written skills and cron jobs run with the agent's privileges, so the checklist says to review them the way you review pull requests; they are not automatically sandboxed. Deleting all of them discards the self-improvement, and --yolo removes the last safety check."}
],
"p12m6":[
 {id:"p12m6q1", q:"A cron job on your Pi runs hermes peer run gpu 'nightly report' with an --idempotency-key, and retries at 7:30 if the 7:00 run fails. Which key would fail to prevent a duplicate job?", options:["report-$(date +%F), the same value for the whole day","report-$(date +%s), the current Unix time in seconds","report-$(hostname)-$(date +%F), host name plus the date","A per-day string written to a file at 7:00 and reused on retry"], answer:1, why:"The key prevents duplicates only if the retry sends the same value, so the receiver recognizes it and does not run the job twice. date +%s changes every second, so the 7:30 retry looks like a brand-new job. Date-based keys stay constant all day, which is the point."},
 {id:"p12m6q2", q:"Two profiles on the same laptop, 'research' and 'writer', need to hand work to each other. Following the docs' advice, which mechanism should you use?", options:["A2A, binding each profile's endpoint to 0.0.0.0 on port 9900","hermes peer, adding each profile as a peer of the other","Kanban or in-process delegation, keeping A2A for other machines","The hermes mesh init command, which links local profiles together"], answer:2, why:"The docs say to prefer in-process delegation or Kanban for agents on the same machine and reserve A2A for crossing machines or vendors. hermes mesh init is an unimplemented RFC, and exposing A2A on 0.0.0.0 adds network risk for no benefit."},
 {id:"p12m6q3", q:"You want every A2A agent that claims the 'code-review' capability to review the same diff, so you can compare their findings side by side. Which call fits?", options:["a2a_orchestrate('code-review', diff, mode=all)","a2a_orchestrate('code-review', diff, mode=first)","a2a_orchestrate('code-review', diff, mode=best)","a2a_discover('code-review') with the diff attached"], answer:0, why:"a2a_orchestrate fans a task out to every agent claiming a capability, and mode=all returns every answer, which is what a comparison needs. mode=best returns one chosen answer and mode=first only the fastest. a2a_discover takes a URL to find an agent; it sends no work."},
 {id:"p12m6q4", q:"The parent agent uses a frontier model. You hand 12 file summaries to delegate_task, each taking about 3 minutes, at the default concurrency. Roughly how long, and how do you cut the cost?", options:["About 3 minutes, since all 12 run at once; cost is fixed by the parent","About 36 minutes, since subagents run one at a time on the parent's model","About 9 minutes; set delegation.model to the frontier model for accuracy","About 12 minutes; set delegation.model to a cheaper or local model"], answer:3, why:"Three subagents run concurrently by default, so 12 tasks take four rounds of about 3 minutes, roughly 12 minutes. delegation.model lets subagents use a cheaper or local model while the parent keeps the strong one: route each task to the cheapest capable brain."},
 {id:"p12m6q5", q:"Your home PC and a friend's PC sit behind different home routers with no VPN between them. Which existing component lets the two Hermes installs reach each other by public key, with NAT traversal?", options:["The hermes mesh init command described in the Hermes Mesh RFC","The hermes-iroh-interconnect plugin from the official catalog","A2A with its default binding of 127.0.0.1 on port 9900","The hermes-mesh PyPI package's mesh_send tool on its own"], answer:1, why:"hermes-iroh-interconnect uses iroh: dial by public key over QUIC with hole-punching and relay fallback. hermes mesh init is an unimplemented proposal, A2A on 127.0.0.1 accepts only local connections, and the hermes-mesh relay needs a peer registry rather than providing NAT traversal."}
],
"p12m7":[
 {id:"p12m7q1", q:"Your two-machine split-mode mesh on wired gigabit gives usable speed. You add a third machine, connected over Wi-Fi, to hold some of the layers. What should you expect?", options:["Faster tokens, since three GPUs now share the work of every token","The same speed, since activations cross the network only once per prompt","Room for a bigger model, but slower tokens from an extra hop over Wi-Fi","No change, since split mode keeps all of its traffic inside one layer"], answer:2, why:"Pipeline parallelism enables size, not speed: each token's activations cross every range boundary, so another node adds a hop, and Wi-Fi adds latency and jitter. Expecting more GPUs to mean faster tokens is the misconception the lesson warns about."},
 {id:"p12m7q2", q:"The invite token for your private mesh was pasted into a group chat by mistake. You want only nodes you own to take part. What actually enforces that?", options:["Nothing more is needed; the token itself proves a node may join","Setting --mesh-discovery-mode mdns so outsiders cannot find the mesh","A shorter --publish time-to-live so the leaked token expires sooner","Running auth init, signing your nodes, and --trust-policy require-owned"], answer:3, why:"The docs call the invite token connectivity material, not an authorization credential; it helps peers find and reach the mesh. Authorization comes from the trust system: auth init, sign-node and --trust-policy require-owned or allowlist. mDNS limits discovery but proves nothing about ownership."},
 {id:"p12m7q3", q:"To save money you join the public mesh with serve --auto. A client asks you to summarize their confidential contract. Is it safe to send it through that mesh?", options:["Yes, because QUIC encryption hides the prompt from every peer","No, because the peer serving the request necessarily sees the prompt","Yes, because relays forward the traffic but cannot read inference data","No, because the public mesh stores every prompt on the blackboard"], answer:1, why:"On the public mesh your prompt runs on a stranger's GPU, and the serving peer must see it to compute the answer. Relays really cannot read the traffic, which makes the 'yes' options tempting, but encryption in transit does not hide data from the machine doing the inference."},
 {id:"p12m7q4", q:"Why does Mesh LLM's split mode cut a big model into contiguous layer ranges across machines, instead of splitting each layer across devices as tensor parallelism does?", options:["No cross-device traffic is needed within a layer, which suits home networks","Each device needs less memory than it would under tensor parallelism","It removes all per-token network traffic, so speed matches one machine","Layer ranges let relays cache activations and replay them between peers"], answer:0, why:"Pipeline parallelism needs zero inter-device communication within a layer during decoding; only activations at range boundaries cross the network, which ordinary home links can carry. The cost is higher per-device memory for the range each node owns, so the 'less memory' option is backwards."},
 {id:"p12m7q5", q:"Your laptop runs mesh-llm client --discover my-mesh, and mesh-llm hermes --write pointed Hermes at it. Chats work, but the laptop's GPU stays idle the whole time. What explains it?", options:["The laptop has the wrong release flavor and needs the cuda build","The laptop is in split mode, waiting to be assigned its layer range","Client mode is API-only, so serving nodes do the inference for it","Hermes is bypassing Mesh LLM and calling a cloud provider instead"], answer:2, why:"mesh-llm client joins the mesh as an API-only client that does no GPU work; requests to localhost:9337/v1 are routed to nodes running serve. A wrong flavor would show up in mesh-llm gpus, but an idle GPU here is simply how client mode behaves."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p13m1":[
 {id:"p13m1q1", q:"A nested-loop duplicate check does about n² simple operations in compiled C at 10^9 operations per second. How long does it take for n = 300,000, and roughly how long would an n log n sort-then-scan take?", options:["About 90 seconds, versus about 5 milliseconds","About 9 seconds, versus about 0.5 milliseconds","About 25 hours, versus about 5 seconds","About 0.3 milliseconds, since n is only 300,000"], answer:0, why:"n² = 9 × 10^10 operations, which at 10^9 per second is 90 s. n log2 n ≈ 300,000 × 18 ≈ 5.4 × 10^6 operations, about 5 ms. The 0.3 ms option counts n instead of n²; that is the cost of one linear scan, not of the nested loop."},
 {id:"p13m1q2", q:"A de-duplication starts with out = [] and, for each x in items, runs: if x not in out: out.append(x). It takes 2 s on 20,000 mostly distinct items. Predict the time for 80,000 items and name the cause.", options:["About 8 s; a single loop over the items makes it linear","About 8 s; append is amortized Θ(1), so the loop is Θ(n)","About 32 s; 'x not in out' scans the list, so it is Θ(n²)","About 2.5 s; the membership test gets cached as the list grows"], answer:2, why:"'x not in out' is a linear scan of a list, so the loop hides a quadratic: four times the input means about sixteen times the work, 2 s × 16 = 32 s. Append really is amortized Θ(1), which makes the linear guess tempting, but the membership test dominates. A set fixes it."},
 {id:"p13m1q3", q:"The code sets best = a[0], then for i in range(1, n) runs: if a[i] > best: best = a[i]. Which loop invariant, true at the start of each iteration, proves that best is the maximum of a when the loop ends?", options:["best is greater than or equal to every element of a","best equals the maximum of a[0..i−1], the elements seen so far","best equals the maximum of a[0..i], including a[i]","best equals a[i−1], the element that the loop examined most recently"], answer:1, why:"At the start of iteration i the loop has examined a[0..i−1]; it holds initially (best = a[0] at i = 1), each iteration maintains it, and at exit i = n, giving the maximum of the whole array. Including a[i] is the off-by-one trap: a[i] has not been compared yet."},
 {id:"p13m1q4", q:"A real-time audio callback must finish within 2 ms. It appends each sample to a Python list that grows for the whole session, and every so often an audible glitch occurs. What explains it, and what is the fix?", options:["Amortized Θ(1) makes every append constant, so the list is not the cause","Python lists are linked lists, so each append walks to the end first","Appends are Θ(n) on average, so insert at the front of the list instead","A rare resize copies the whole list; preallocate a fixed-size ring buffer"], answer:3, why:"Amortized Θ(1) is an average over a sequence: most appends are cheap, but a resize occasionally copies every element, a Θ(n) pause. A real-time deadline cannot absorb that, so preallocate fixed storage. Reading amortized as a per-operation guarantee is the tempting mistake."},
 {id:"p13m1q5", q:"The code sets i = 1; then, while i < n, it calls work() i times (for j in range(i)) and afterwards doubles i. How many times does work() run in total, as a function of n?", options:["Θ(n), because 1 + 2 + 4 + … stays below 2n","Θ(n log n), because the outer loop runs log n times","Θ(n²), because nested loops multiply their counts","Θ(log n), because i doubles on every pass"], answer:0, why:"The outer loop runs about log2 n times, but the inner loop runs only i times with i = 1, 2, 4, …, so the total is a geometric sum below 2n, the same sum as array doubling. Multiplying log n by n overcounts, because most passes are short."}
],
"p13m2":[
 {id:"p13m2q1", q:"An open-addressing hash table with linear probing is 75% full. About how many probes does an unsuccessful search expect, and how does that compare with the same table at 50% full?", options:["About 1.75 probes, since the cost is roughly 1 + α","About 4 probes, since the cost is roughly 1/(1 − α)","About 50 probes, the cost once a table passes two thirds full","About 8.5 probes, versus about 2.5 at half full"], answer:3, why:"For linear probing an unsuccessful search costs about (1 + 1/(1 − α)²)/2. At α = 0.75 that is (1 + 16)/2 = 8.5; at 0.5 it is 2.5. The 1 + α formula is for chaining, which is why it tempts, and the steep curve is why tables resize early."},
 {id:"p13m2q2", q:"Which of these workloads is one where a doubly linked list is genuinely the right structure?", options:["An LRU cache where a dict holds each entry's node, moved to the front on use","A first-in, first-out queue holding up to five million incoming network packets","A table of one million sensor values read by index in random order","A sequence of ten million integers summed as fast as possible"], answer:0, why:"Linked lists win when you already hold the node you will modify; the dict supplies it, so unlinking and relinking are Θ(1). A queue is better as a ring buffer or deque, indexing needs an array, and a sequential sum wants contiguous memory, not a chain of cache misses."},
 {id:"p13m2q3", q:"You hold 10 million distinct integers, all above one million, in memory. Roughly how much RAM does a Python list of them use compared with a NumPy int64 array?", options:["About 80 MB either way, since both store 8-byte integers","About 280 MB as a list and about 280 MB in NumPy","About 360 MB as a list, about 80 MB as a NumPy array","About 36 MB as a list, about 8 MB as a NumPy array"], answer:2, why:"A list stores 8-byte pointers (80 MB) to separate 28-byte int objects (280 MB), about 360 MB in total. NumPy stores the raw 8-byte values contiguously: 80 MB. Treating a list as contiguous integers is the tempting error; it is an array of pointers."},
 {id:"p13m2q4", q:"A C program sums an 8,000 × 8,000 matrix of doubles stored row by row. Version A loops over rows outside and columns inside; version B swaps the loops. Both do 64 million additions. What do you predict?", options:["Same speed, since both are Θ(n²) with identical operation counts","A is several times faster, because it walks each 64-byte cache line in order","B is faster, because the prefetcher prefers jumping between rows","A is faster, but only because it executes fewer loop-control instructions per element"], answer:1, why:"Row-major storage puts a row's elements side by side, so A uses all eight doubles in each 64-byte line and the prefetcher streams ahead. B jumps 64,000 bytes per step and touches a new line every time, which the lesson puts at up to ten times slower. Big-O hides this constant."},
 {id:"p13m2q5", q:"You hash keys into 2^20 (about one million) buckets using a good hash function. Roughly how many insertions until you should expect the first collision?", options:["About 1,300, from the birthday bound √(πm/2)","About 524,000, once half of the buckets are in use","About 1,048,576, only after every bucket holds a key","Never, because a good hash function avoids collisions"], answer:0, why:"With m buckets the first collision is expected after about √(πm/2) insertions: √(1.57 × 1,048,576) ≈ 1,280. Collisions are guaranteed by the pigeonhole principle and arrive far earlier than intuition says; a good hash spreads them evenly and unpredictably, it does not prevent them."}
],
"p13m3":[
 {id:"p13m3q1", q:"An algorithm splits a problem of size n into 4 subproblems of size n/2 and spends n² time combining their results. By the master theorem, what is T(n) = 4T(n/2) + n²?", options:["Θ(n²), because the combining work dominates","Θ(n² log n), because leaf work and combining work match","Θ(n log n), as for every halving divide and conquer","Θ(n³), because four subproblems cube the growth"], answer:1, why:"Here n^(log2 4) = n², which equals f(n) = n², so the equal case applies: Θ(n² log n), with about n² work on each of log n levels. Θ(n²) misreads it as the combine-dominates case, which needs f(n) to grow strictly faster than n²."},
 {id:"p13m3q2", q:"def count(node): return 0 if node is None else 1 + count(node.next) — this Python function passes every unit test but crashes on a 50,000-node linked list. What is the cause, and the right fix?", options:["The list must contain a cycle; add memoization with functools.cache","Rewrite it in tail-recursive form so Python reuses the stack frame","Raise the recursion limit to ten million, which is always safe","Depth-n recursion passes Python's ~1,000-frame limit; use a loop"], answer:3, why:"Each call pushes a stack frame, so a 50,000-node list needs 50,000 frames, far past Python's default limit of about 1,000. Python does not eliminate tail calls, so a tail-recursive rewrite still fails; a recursion that is really a loop should be written as a loop."},
 {id:"p13m3q3", q:"A quicksort that always takes the first element as its pivot is given 100,000 records that are already sorted. What happens, and what does the lesson recommend?", options:["Θ(n²): one-sided splits, about 5 × 10^9 comparisons; use a random pivot or introsort","About 1.7 × 10^6 comparisons, since quicksort always runs in n log n time, whatever the input","Θ(n), because already-sorted input needs no swaps during partitioning","Balanced splits, but the order of equal keys is lost; switch to heapsort"], answer:0, why:"With the first element as pivot, sorted input puts everything on one side, so the work sums to about n²/2 ≈ 5 × 10^9 comparisons. n log n is only the expected cost with good pivots, which is why C++ uses introsort, switching to heapsort when recursion gets too deep."},
 {id:"p13m3q4", q:"You radix-sort 10 million 32-bit integers using 16-bit digits (two passes, 65,536 digit values). Roughly how many basic operations is that, compared with an n log2 n comparison sort?", options:["Impossible, because no sort can beat the Ω(n log n) lower bound","About 4 × 10^7, from four passes over 8-bit digits","About 2 × 10^7, roughly 11 times fewer than n log2 n ≈ 2.3 × 10^8","About 2.3 × 10^8, the same as any well-implemented sort"], answer:2, why:"Radix sort costs d(n + k) = 2 × (10^7 + 65,536) ≈ 2 × 10^7, against 10^7 × log2(10^7) ≈ 2.3 × 10^8 for comparisons. The lower bound covers only comparison sorts; reading digits changes the model, so the 'impossible' option misapplies it."},
 {id:"p13m3q5", q:"In the worst case, at least how many comparisons must any comparison-based sort make to sort 5 distinct items?", options:["At least 7, since log2(5!) = log2(120) ≈ 6.9","At least 5, one comparison per item","At least 10, one comparison per pair of items","At least 12, since n log2 n ≈ 11.6"], answer:0, why:"A correct comparison sort needs a decision tree with a leaf for each of the 5! = 120 orderings, and a binary tree with 120 leaves has height at least ⌈log2 120⌉ = 7. The 12 option uses n log2 n, which gives the asymptotic shape, not the exact bound."}
],
"p13m4":[
 {id:"p13m4q1", q:"A PostgreSQL index uses 8 KB pages; each entry is a 16-byte key plus an 8-byte child pointer. About how many levels does the B-tree need to index 500 million keys?", options:["2 levels, since each page holds hundreds of entries","3 levels, since the fan-out cubed easily covers that many keys","4 levels: fan-out about 341, and 341³ ≈ 40 million is too few","29 levels, since log2 of 500 million is about 29"], answer:2, why:"Fan-out ≈ 8,192 ÷ 24 ≈ 341. Three levels index 341³ ≈ 40 million keys, too few for 500 million; four levels reach about 13.5 billion. The 29-level answer is a binary tree's height, exactly the per-level I/O cost that page-sized B-tree nodes avoid."},
 {id:"p13m4q2", q:"A min-heap is stored in the array [2, 5, 3, 9, 6, 4]. You insert 1 and sift it up. What does the array look like afterwards?", options:["[1, 5, 2, 9, 6, 4, 3]","[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 9]","[1, 5, 3, 9, 6, 4, 2]","[2, 5, 3, 9, 6, 4, 1]"], answer:0, why:"1 is appended at index 6, whose parent is (6 − 1) // 2 = 2 (value 3), so they swap; its new parent is index 0 (value 2), so they swap again. A heap only orders parents over children, so a fully sorted array is not what an insertion produces."},
 {id:"p13m4q3", q:"A router's table holds 10.0.0.0/8 → A, 10.1.0.0/16 → B, 10.1.2.0/24 → C and 0.0.0.0/0 → D. A packet arrives for 10.1.3.7. Which next hop wins?", options:["A, because 10.0.0.0/8 is the first route that matches","B, the longest prefix that actually matches the address","C, because /24 is the most specific route in the table","D, because the default route matches every address"], answer:1, why:"10.1.3.7 matches /0, /8 and /16, but not 10.1.2.0/24, whose third octet is 2. Longest-prefix match picks the most specific route that matches, so B wins. C tempts because it is the most specific entry overall, but it does not cover this address."},
 {id:"p13m4q4", q:"A fraud system stores 50 million transactions keyed by timestamp and must answer 'every transaction between 02:00 and 02:15' and 'the first one after time t'. Which structure fits best?", options:["A hash table keyed by timestamp, for Θ(1) expected lookups","A binary min-heap keyed by timestamp, for fast access to the minimum","An unsorted array scanned on each query, since it is cache-friendly","A balanced tree or B+-tree on timestamp, then walk the range in order"], answer:3, why:"Ordered structures answer range and successor queries: descend in Θ(log n), then walk keys in order. A hash table gives fast exact lookups but no order, so it cannot find 'the next one after t'. A heap only exposes its minimum, and a full scan is Θ(n) per query."},
 {id:"p13m4q5", q:"A naive trie for a word list ends up with about 400,000 nodes, each holding 27 child pointers of 8 bytes. Roughly how much memory do the pointers alone take?", options:["About 86 MB, far more than a well-sized hash table","About 11 MB, at 27 bytes per node for the 27 child slots","About 3.2 MB, one 8-byte pointer per node","About 400 KB, one byte per node"], answer:0, why:"Each node holds 27 × 8 = 216 bytes of pointers, and 400,000 × 216 ≈ 86 MB, against roughly 10 MB for the lesson's chained hash table. The 11 MB option forgets that pointers are 8 bytes. A trie avoids collisions but pays in memory and cache misses."}
],
"p13m5":[
 {id:"p13m5q1", q:"Undirected weighted graph: S-A 7, S-B 2, B-A 3, A-C 1, B-C 8, A-D 6, C-D 2. Running Dijkstra's algorithm from S, what is the shortest distance to D, and by which path?", options:["8, via S-B-A-C-D","10, via S-A-C-D","11, via S-B-A-D","12, via S-B-C-D"], answer:0, why:"Settle S (0), then B (2), which lowers A to 5; settling A lowers C to 6 and sets D to 11; settling C lowers D to 8. S-B-A-C-D has the most hops but the least total weight. The S-A route looks direct, but its first edge alone costs 7."},
 {id:"p13m5q2", q:"You model fiber leases as a weighted directed graph, and one link carries a rebate, so its weight is −4. You need the cheapest cost from the head end to every site. Which algorithm, and why?", options:["Dijkstra, because a binary heap makes it the fastest choice on any set of edge weights","Prim, because the cheapest network to every site is the MST","Bellman-Ford, since a negative edge can make Dijkstra's settled values wrong","BFS, because it finds shortest paths in Θ(V + E) time"], answer:2, why:"Dijkstra assumes a settled distance can never improve, which a negative edge breaks. Bellman-Ford relaxes every edge V − 1 times, tolerates negative weights, and one more pass detects a negative cycle. BFS counts hops rather than weights, and an MST answers a different question."},
 {id:"p13m5q3", q:"Undirected weighted graph: S-A 7, S-B 2, B-A 3, A-C 1, B-C 8, A-D 6, C-D 2. Which edge set does Kruskal's algorithm choose for the minimum spanning tree?", options:["S-B, B-A, A-D, A-C, for a total of 12","A-C, S-B, C-D, S-A, for a total of 12","A-C, S-B, C-D, B-A, A-D, for a total of 14","A-C, S-B, C-D, B-A, for a total of 8"], answer:3, why:"In sorted order A-C 1, S-B 2, C-D 2 and B-A 3 are accepted; A-D 6, S-A 7 and B-C 8 each join vertices already in one component, so union-find rejects them. Five vertices need exactly four edges, so the five-edge set has a cycle; the weight-12 trees span but are not minimal."},
 {id:"p13m5q4", q:"You are modeling 2 million routers with about 6 links each as a graph. Which representation do you choose, and why?", options:["An adjacency matrix, since its Θ(1) edge lookup outweighs the extra memory it needs","Adjacency lists: about 14 million entries, versus 4 × 10^12 matrix cells","Either one, since both use Θ(V + E) memory on sparse graphs","An adjacency matrix, since sparse graphs compress well inside it"], answer:1, why:"A V × V matrix has (2 × 10^6)² = 4 × 10^12 cells, about 500 GB even at one bit each. Adjacency lists need Θ(V + E): 2 million vertices plus about 12 million neighbor entries for 6 million undirected links. Fast edge lookup cannot justify memory you cannot buy."},
 {id:"p13m5q5", q:"A monitoring tool receives 10 million link-up events and must answer 10 million 'are host X and host Y connected?' queries interleaved with them. Which approach scales?", options:["Run a fresh BFS from X for every query, at Θ(V + E) each","Keep an adjacency matrix and look up the edge (X, Y) directly for every query","Union-find with rank and path compression, near-constant per operation","Rerun Kruskal's algorithm from scratch after every new link"], answer:2, why:"Union-find merges components on each link-up and answers a query with two finds, each amortized α(n), at most 4 in practice. A BFS per query repeats Θ(V + E) work millions of times, and a matrix lookup only reports a direct link, not a path through other hosts."}
],
"p13m6":[
 {id:"p13m6q1", q:"Pick the most non-overlapping meetings from: A 9:00–12:00, B 9:30–10:00, C 10:00–10:30, D 10:30–11:00, E 11:30–13:00. Which greedy rule is provably optimal, and what does it pick?", options:["Earliest start first: it picks A, which blocks the rest, so 1","Earliest finish first: it picks B, C, D and E, so 4","Shortest first is the proven rule: it picks B, C, D, then E","Longest first: it picks A and then E, so 2 meetings"], answer:1, why:"Earliest finishing time has an exchange-argument proof: the first meeting to finish can replace the first meeting of any optimal schedule. Shortest-first happens to pick 4 here but has counterexamples, so working on one case is no proof. A and E overlap, and earliest-start is trapped by A."},
 {id:"p13m6q2", q:"A phishing filter flags any domain within edit distance 1 of a protected brand. Compared with amazon.com, does it catch arnazon.com?", options:["No, the distance is 2: substitute m→r and insert n","Yes, the distance is 1, since 'rn' counts as one edit for 'm'","No, the distance is 3: delete m, then insert r and n","No, the distance is about 10, since every later letter shifts"], answer:0, why:"Edit distance counts single-character insertions, deletions and substitutions; turning m into rn takes one substitution and one insertion, so the minimum is 2. Delete-plus-two-inserts is a valid path but not minimal. 'rn' looks like one letter to a human, so this filter needs a threshold of 2."},
 {id:"p13m6q3", q:"A 0/1 knapsack DP over 200 items with a capacity of 50,000 kg runs in 0.1 s. The customer switches units to grams, so the capacity becomes 50,000,000. What happens to the running time?", options:["Unchanged, because the cost is polynomial in the number of items","About 3× slower, because W gained only three more digits","About 10× slower, because only the bit length of W changed","About 1,000× slower, roughly 100 s, since the cost is Θ(nW)"], answer:3, why:"The table has n × W cells and W grew 1,000×, so time (and memory) grow about 1,000×. Three more digits is only about 10 more bits, which is exactly why Θ(nW) is called pseudo-polynomial: it is exponential in the length of W, not polynomial."},
 {id:"p13m6q4", q:"Knapsack capacity 10 kg. Items: X 6 kg worth $36, Y 5 kg worth $25, Z 5 kg worth $25. A greedy rule takes items in order of value per kg. Which statement is correct?", options:["Greedy is optimal for the 0/1 version, giving $36","Greedy gives $61 by taking X and Y together","0/1: greedy gets $36 but Y + Z gets $50; with fractions, greedy is optimal","Greedy gets $50, because Y and Z have equal value ratios and fill the sack exactly"], answer:2, why:"Greedy takes X ($6/kg) first, leaving 4 kg where neither 5 kg item fits: $36. The DP finds Y + Z for $50. Fractional knapsack has the greedy-choice property, so X plus 4 kg of Y ($56) is optimal there; the 0/1 version lacks it, and X + Y weighs 11 kg."},
 {id:"p13m6q5", q:"You diff two log files of 30,000 and 40,000 lines with a full LCS table, at about 10^9 cell updates per second and 4 bytes per cell. What does it cost?", options:["About 1.2 × 10^9 cells: roughly 1 s, but about 4.8 GB to keep the whole table","About 70,000 cells, since the cost is m + n: a few microseconds","About 1.2 × 10^9 cells: roughly 20 minutes of compute, using only a few MB of memory","About 2^70,000 steps, since every subsequence must be compared"], answer:0, why:"LCS has m × n = 30,000 × 40,000 = 1.2 × 10^9 states at Θ(1) each, about a second of compute, but storing every cell at 4 bytes takes 4.8 GB. Keeping only the last row saves memory at the cost of easy reconstruction. Enumerating subsequences is the brute force DP avoids."}
],
"p13m7":[
 {id:"p13m7q1", q:"What is the minimum number of states for a DFA that accepts exactly the binary strings in which the number of 1s is divisible by 3?", options:["2 states, one accepting and one rejecting","4 states, one for each count from 0 to 3","3 states, one per remainder of the count mod 3","None, because counting needs a stack, so no DFA exists"], answer:2, why:"The machine only needs the count of 1s modulo 3, which has three values: each 1 moves to the next remainder, each 0 stays put, and remainder 0 accepts. Unbounded counting needs a stack, but counting modulo a fixed number fits in finite states, so the 'no DFA' option is wrong."},
 {id:"p13m7q2", q:"You are reviewing four input-validation patterns in a service that uses Python's backtracking re module. Which one is prone to catastrophic backtracking (ReDoS)?", options:["^\\d{1,10}$","^(cat|dog|bird)$","^[a-z0-9]{3,20}$","^(\\d+)*$"], answer:3, why:"^(\\d+)*$ nests one quantifier inside another, so a long digit run followed by a non-digit can be split among the repetitions in exponentially many ways before failing. The others have no overlapping repetition. Fixes: remove the nesting, cap input length, or use a linear-time engine such as RE2."},
 {id:"p13m7q3", q:"A vendor claims its scanner decides, for any program at all, with no false positives, no false negatives and no timeouts, whether that program will ever exfiltrate data. How should you judge the claim?", options:["Impossible in general: Rice's theorem makes such a behavioral property undecidable","Plausible given enough compute, because every program is finite and can be fully checked","Plausible if it uses machine learning trained on enough malware","Plausible, because exfiltration always shows up as a fixed code pattern"], answer:0, why:"Whether a program ever exfiltrates is a non-trivial property of its behavior, so by Rice's theorem no algorithm decides it correctly for every program. Real tools approximate with signatures, heuristics and sandboxes and accept errors or timeouts. More compute or machine learning does not remove undecidability."},
 {id:"p13m7q4", q:"You suspect your new scheduling problem X is NP-hard. What must you show to prove it?", options:["A polynomial reduction from X to SAT, so that a SAT solver can solve every instance of X","A polynomial reduction from a known NP-complete problem, such as 3-SAT, to X","That brute force on X takes 2^n steps on all of your test inputs","That nobody has found a polynomial-time algorithm for X so far"], answer:1, why:"A ≤p B shows B is at least as hard as A, so you reduce a known NP-complete problem to X. Reducing X to SAT is the common reversal: it only shows X is no harder than SAT. Slow brute force, or the lack of a known fast algorithm, proves nothing."},
 {id:"p13m7q5", q:"A service accepts user-supplied records and later uses them to build SQL queries. Which design best follows the LangSec advice from this lesson?", options:["A blocklist regex that strips quotes, semicolons and the word DROP","A flexible, Turing-complete template format so users can express anything","Escaping special characters after the input is joined into the query","Recognizing input with a strict parser for exactly the intended format first"], answer:3, why:"Injection is grammar confusion: data from one language reinterpreted in another. LangSec says recognize input with a parser for exactly the language you intend, reject everything else, and keep formats low in the hierarchy. Blocklists and late escaping guess at the attacker's grammar instead of defining yours."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p14m1":[
 {id:"p14m1q1", q:"Intent: allow SSH only from the VPN (V) and only with MFA (M). An admin writes the deny rule as ¬V ∧ ¬M. Which connections does that rule wrongly let through?", options:["Only V = F, M = F, the outsider without MFA","None, because ¬V ∧ ¬M is already the correct De Morgan negation of the intent V ∧ M","Only V = T, M = T, which the rule wrongly lets through","Both mixed rows (V = T, M = F and V = F, M = T); deny should be ¬V ∨ ¬M"], answer:3, why:"The deny rule must be ¬(V ∧ M), which De Morgan turns into ¬V ∨ ¬M. ¬V ∧ ¬M is false whenever exactly one condition holds, so a VPN user without MFA and an outsider with MFA both get through. Swapping ∧ for ∨ is the mistake De Morgan exists to catch."},
 {id:"p14m1q2", q:"An audit script runs all(requires_auth(e) for e in endpoints) and the result is False. What exactly does that tell you?", options:["No endpoint in the list requires authentication","At least one endpoint does not require authentication","At least one endpoint does require authentication","The endpoints list must have been empty"], answer:1, why:"all() is ∀ over the list, and ¬∀x P(x) ≡ ∃x ¬P(x): one counterexample is enough to make it False. 'No endpoint requires it' would be ∀x ¬P(x), a far stronger claim. An empty list makes all() return True, vacuously, not False."},
 {id:"p14m1q3", q:"Let Opens(k, u) mean 'key k opens user u's account.' Which statement describes a master-key vulnerability?", options:["∀u ∃k Opens(k, u): every user has some key that works","∃u ∃k Opens(k, u): some key opens some account","∃k ∀u Opens(k, u): one key opens every account","∀k ∃u Opens(k, u): every key opens some account"], answer:2, why:"Quantifier order matters. ∃k ∀u says a single key works for every user, which is a master key. ∀u ∃k says only that each user has a working key, possibly a different one each, which is how normal accounts behave. Swapping the order changes the claim entirely."},
 {id:"p14m1q4", q:"A student proves 'if n² is odd, then n is odd' as follows: let n = 2k + 1; then n² = 4k² + 4k + 1, which is odd. What is wrong with the proof?", options:["Nothing; it is a valid direct proof of the stated claim","It proves the converse; assume n is even and show n² is even instead","The claim is about all integers, so it needs a proof by induction","Contrapositive proofs are invalid, so a contradiction proof is required"], answer:1, why:"Starting from n odd and reaching n² odd proves 'n odd → n² odd', the converse. The claim's contrapositive is 'n even → n² even': n = 2k gives n² = 2(2k²). The converse is independent of the claim, so proving it establishes nothing here."},
 {id:"p14m1q5", q:"Which of these relations on IPv4 addresses is an equivalence relation?", options:["'is numerically less than'","'is exactly one router hop away from'","'differs in exactly one bit from'","'is in the same /24 subnet as'"], answer:3, why:"Same /24 is reflexive, symmetric and transitive, so it splits addresses into disjoint classes, one per subnet. 'Differs in one bit' is symmetric but neither reflexive (an address differs from itself in no bits) nor transitive; 'less than' is not symmetric; one hop fails reflexivity and transitivity."}
],
"p14m2":[
 {id:"p14m2q1", q:"Find the inverse of 7 modulo 40, if it exists.", options:["17, because 7 × 17 = 119","23, because 7 × 23 = 161 = 4 × 40 + 1","6, because 7 × 6 = 42 is just above 40","None exists, because 40 is not prime"], answer:1, why:"An inverse exists exactly when gcd(7, 40) = 1, so 40 need not be prime. Extended Euclid gives 1 = 3·40 − 17·7, so 7⁻¹ ≡ −17 ≡ 23 (mod 40); check: 161 = 4 × 40 + 1. 7 × 17 = 119 ≡ −1, the negative of the inverse."},
 {id:"p14m2q2", q:"You generate a 1,024-bit prime by testing random odd 1,024-bit candidates with Miller–Rabin. Roughly how many candidates should you expect to test?", options:["About 2^512, the square root of the candidate size","About 1,420, using the lesson's prime-density figure for numbers near 2^2048","About 355: 1 in ln(2^1024) ≈ 710 numbers is prime, and evens are skipped","About 1,024, one candidate for each bit of the number"], answer:2, why:"By the prime number theorem about 1 in ln n numbers near n is prime; ln(2^1024) = 1,024 × 0.693 ≈ 710. Skipping even numbers doubles the density, so expect about 355 tries. 2^512 is the trial-division cost of factoring, a different and infeasible problem."},
 {id:"p14m2q3", q:"Compute 3^13 mod 17 by square-and-multiply.", options:["13, the value of 3^4 mod 17","16, the value of 3^8 mod 17","5, from 3^4 × 3^1 ≡ 13 × 3 = 39 ≡ 5 (mod 17)","12, from 3^8 × 3^4 × 3 ≡ 16 × 13 × 3"], answer:3, why:"Squaring gives 3^2 = 9, 3^4 = 81 ≡ 13 and 3^8 ≡ 13² = 169 ≡ 16. Because 13 = 8 + 4 + 1, multiply 16 × 13 × 3 = 624 ≡ 12 (mod 17). The answer 5 drops the 8-bit of the exponent; every 1-bit of e must be multiplied in."},
 {id:"p14m2q4", q:"A disassembler shows the 8-bit signed (two's complement) value 0xF6. What number is it?", options:["−10, since 0xF6 = 246 and 246 − 256 = −10","246, since 0xF6 = 15 × 16 + 6","−118, since the low seven bits 0x76 equal 118","−9, since flipping the bits of 0xF6 gives 0x09"], answer:0, why:"Two's complement stores −x as 2^8 − x, so a byte with its top bit set is its unsigned value minus 256: 246 − 256 = −10. −118 reads it as sign-and-magnitude, and −9 stops after flipping the bits; two's complement also adds 1, giving 0x0A = 10."},
 {id:"p14m2q5", q:"An app encrypts two different messages by XOR with the same random key: c₁ = m₁ ⊕ k and c₂ = m₂ ⊕ k. What does anyone holding both ciphertexts learn from c₁ ⊕ c₂?", options:["The key k, since the two messages cancel out","Nothing, because a truly random key hides everything","m₁ AND m₂, the bits the two messages share","m₁ ⊕ m₂, since k ⊕ k = 0 cancels the key"], answer:3, why:"XOR is its own inverse, so c₁ ⊕ c₂ = m₁ ⊕ m₂ ⊕ k ⊕ k = m₁ ⊕ m₂: the key drops out and the relation between the plaintexts leaks. A random key gives perfect secrecy only when used once, which is why a one-time pad must never be reused."}
],
"p14m3":[
 {id:"p14m3q1", q:"You are sizing your own password policy: 10 characters from 62 letters and digits. If the hashes leaked, how long would an exhaustive search take at 10^11 guesses/s (fast unsalted hash), and at 10^5/s (bcrypt)?", options:["About 97 days at 10^11/s, about 266,000 years at 10^5/s","About 24 minutes at 10^11/s, about 45 years at 10^5/s","Under a microsecond, since there are only 62 × 10 = 620 choices","About 97 days either way, since the search space is unchanged"], answer:0, why:"The product rule gives 62^10 ≈ 8.4 × 10^17 candidates: 8.4 × 10^6 s ≈ 97 days at 10^11 per second and 8.4 × 10^12 s ≈ 266,000 years at 10^5. The space is the same, but slow hashing cuts the rate a millionfold. The 24-minute figure counts only 26 lowercase letters."},
 {id:"p14m3q2", q:"A door keypad code is 3 different buttons out of 12, pressed in a set order. How many codes are possible, and how many would there be if the lock ignored the order?", options:["1,728 in order; 220 if order is ignored","220 in order; 1,320 if order is ignored","1,320 in order; 220 if order is ignored","1,320 in order; 440 if order is ignored"], answer:2, why:"Ordered selections without repeats are P(12, 3) = 12 × 11 × 10 = 1,320. Ignoring order divides by the 3! = 6 orderings of each set: C(12, 3) = 220. 1,728 = 12³ counts codes that may repeat a button, which the rule forbids."},
 {id:"p14m3q3", q:"How many 6-character passwords over lowercase letters and digits (36 symbols) contain at least one digit?", options:["6 × 10 × 36^5 ≈ 3.6 × 10^9, a digit placed in any one of the six positions","36^6 − 26^6 ≈ 1.87 × 10^9, all strings minus those with no digit","10 × 36^5 ≈ 6.0 × 10^8, one digit first and then anything","26^6 ≈ 3.1 × 10^8, the passwords made only of letters"], answer:1, why:"Complement counting: all 36^6 ≈ 2.18 × 10^9 strings minus the 26^6 ≈ 3.09 × 10^8 with no digit leaves about 1.87 × 10^9. The 6 × 10 × 36^5 count is tempting but counts a password with several digits once per digit, so it overshoots."},
 {id:"p14m3q4", q:"Your fleet gives each device a random 48-bit ID. Around how many devices give a 50% chance that two of them share an ID?", options:["About 20 million, from 1.177 × √(2^48) = 1.177 × 2^24","About 1.4 × 10^14, half of the 2^48 possible IDs","About 2.8 × 10^14, once every possible ID is in use","About 77,000, the figure for a 32-bit space"], answer:0, why:"The birthday bound puts a 50% collision chance at about 1.177√N samples; √(2^48) = 2^24 ≈ 16.8 million, times 1.177 ≈ 19.7 million. Half the space is the intuitive answer and is wrong by a factor of millions, because collisions come from pairs, which grow as the square of the count."},
 {id:"p14m3q5", q:"A vendor advertises a lossless compressor that makes every input file at least one byte smaller. What does the pigeonhole principle say about the claim?", options:["It is plausible for text and fails only on video files","It holds, so running it repeatedly would shrink files further on each pass","It is impossible: there are fewer shorter strings than n-byte files","It is possible with a large enough shared dictionary"], answer:2, why:"There are 256^n files of n bytes but fewer strictly shorter strings, so a map that shortens every file must send two files to one output and cannot be reversed. A lossless compressor that shrinks some inputs must expand others; random or encrypted data does not compress."}
],
"p14m4":[
 {id:"p14m4q1", q:"A retrieval system compares the query q = (1, 0, 1) with chunks A = (2, 0, 2), B = (1, 1, 0) and C = (5, 5, 5). Which chunk ranks first by cosine similarity, and which by raw dot product?", options:["C both ways, since it has the largest components","A by cosine (1.0); C by raw dot product (10)","A both ways, since it points the same way as q","B by cosine, since it shares a component with q; A by dot product"], answer:1, why:"Cosine divides by both lengths: A points exactly along q (cosine 1), C scores about 0.82 and B 0.5. The raw dot product rewards magnitude, so C's large components give it 10 against A's 4. That is why retrieval normalizes vectors before ranking."},
 {id:"p14m4q2", q:"R rotates the plane 90° counterclockwise (columns (0, 1) and (−1, 0)); S is the shear with columns (1, 0) and (1, 1). Where does the point (1, 0) land if you rotate then shear, versus shear then rotate?", options:["(1, 1) versus (0, 1), so SR ≠ RS","(0, 1) both ways, since composition commutes","(1, 1) both ways, since both maps are linear","(0, 1) versus (1, 1), so SR ≠ RS"], answer:0, why:"Rotating sends (1, 0) to (0, 1), and the shear sends (0, 1) to its second column, (1, 1). Shearing first leaves (1, 0) unchanged, and rotating then gives (0, 1). Matrix multiplication is composition, so order matters; mixing up which map acts first gives the reversed option."},
 {id:"p14m4q3", q:"A = [[1, 2], [2, 4]] (rows). What can you say about the systems Ax = (3, 6) and Ax = (3, 7)?", options:["det A = 8, so each system has exactly one solution","det A = 0, so neither system has any solution","det A = 0: (3, 6) has infinitely many solutions, (3, 7) has none","det A = 0, but np.linalg.inv still gives one solution to each"], answer:2, why:"det A = 1·4 − 2·2 = 0: the second row is twice the first, so A has rank 1 and squashes the plane onto a line. (3, 6) lies on that line, so every x with x₁ + 2x₂ = 3 works; (3, 7) does not, so nothing maps there. A singular matrix has no inverse."},
 {id:"p14m4q4", q:"For A = [[4, 1], [2, 3]] (rows), which of these vectors is an eigenvector, and with what eigenvalue?", options:["(1, 0), with eigenvalue 4","(1, 2), with eigenvalue 6","(1, −1), with eigenvalue 3","(1, 1), with eigenvalue 5"], answer:3, why:"A(1, 1) = (5, 5) = 5(1, 1), so the direction is unchanged and λ = 5. A(1, 0) = (4, 2), A(1, 2) = (6, 8) and A(1, −1) = (3, −1) all change direction. Check: trace 7 = 5 + 2 and determinant 10 = 5 × 2, so the other eigenvalue is 2."},
 {id:"p14m4q5", q:"Transformer attention computes QKᵀ, every query dotted with every key. If you raise the context from 8,192 to 32,768 tokens, roughly how much does that product's cost grow?", options:["4×, because the context is four times longer","2×, because only the number of keys grows","16×, because the cost grows with the square of the context length","64×, because every matrix product costs n³ when the context holds n tokens"], answer:2, why:"QKᵀ produces one score for every query–key pair, an L × L matrix, so its cost scales with L². Four times the context means 4² = 16 times the work, which is why long context windows are expensive. The n³ rule is for multiplying two square n × n matrices."}
],
"p14m5":[
 {id:"p14m5q1", q:"A phishing detector catches 90% of phishing emails and wrongly flags 5% of legitimate ones; 2% of incoming mail is phishing. An email is flagged. About how likely is it to be phishing?", options:["About 27%, from 0.018 ÷ (0.018 + 0.049)","About 90%, the detector's detection rate","About 95%, one minus the false-alarm rate","About 2%, the base rate, since a flag carries no information"], answer:0, why:"True positives are 0.9 × 0.02 = 0.018 and false positives 0.05 × 0.98 = 0.049, so P(phish | flag) = 0.018 ÷ 0.067 ≈ 27%. Quoting 90% confuses P(flag | phish) with P(phish | flag), the base-rate fallacy; the flag does raise the probability well above 2%."},
 {id:"p14m5q2", q:"Two backup drives each fail with probability 3% per year. A colleague writes P(both fail) = 0.03 × 0.03 = 0.09%. The drives come from one batch and share one power supply. What is the key problem?", options:["It should be 0.03 + 0.03 = 6%, since two drives double the risk","It should be 1 − 0.97² ≈ 5.9%, the chance that both drives fail","Nothing; the product rule is exact once each drive's individual failure rate is known","It assumes independence, but shared batch and power make joint failure likelier"], answer:3, why:"P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B) holds only for independent events. Drives from one batch on one power supply share causes of failure, so the joint probability is likely far above 0.09%. 1 − 0.97² ≈ 5.9% is the chance that at least one fails, a different event."},
 {id:"p14m5q3", q:"A breach has a 5% yearly chance and would cost $400,000. A control costing $15,000 per year cuts the chance to 1%. Judged by expected annual loss, is the control worth buying?", options:["Yes: expected loss falls from $20,000 to $4,000, saving $16,000 for $15,000","No: the remaining $4,000 of expected loss is already less than the $15,000 cost","Yes: the control prevents a $400,000 loss, far more than it costs","No: a 5% chance is too small to justify any spending at all"], answer:0, why:"Annual loss expectancy is probability × impact: 0.05 × $400,000 = $20,000 before and 0.01 × $400,000 = $4,000 after, a $16,000 reduction that narrowly exceeds the $15,000 cost. Comparing the leftover $4,000 with the cost compares the wrong numbers; the benefit is the reduction."},
 {id:"p14m5q4", q:"An 8-drive array has drives that fail independently, each with probability 0.05 this year. What is the probability that exactly 2 of them fail?", options:["About 0.25%, from 0.05²","About 7%, from C(8, 2) × 0.05² over the 28 possible pairs","About 10%, from 2 × 0.05","About 5.1%, from C(8, 2) × 0.05² × 0.95⁶"], answer:3, why:"The binomial gives C(8, 2) p² (1 − p)⁶ = 28 × 0.0025 × 0.735 ≈ 0.051. The 7% option counts which two fail but forgets that the other six must survive, the (1 − p)⁶ factor; 0.05² alone ignores the 28 ways to choose the failing pair."},
 {id:"p14m5q5", q:"A Monte Carlo estimate of π with 10,000 random points is typically off by about 0.016. About how many points do you need to bring the typical error down to 0.0016?", options:["About 100,000 points, ten times as many","About 1,000,000 points, since error shrinks like 1/√n","About 20,000 points, since doubling n halves the error","About 10,000,000 points, a thousand times as many"], answer:1, why:"Typical error falls like 1/√n (here 1.6/√n): √10,000 = 100 gives 0.016 and √1,000,000 = 1,000 gives 0.0016. Ten times the accuracy costs a hundred times the samples; ten times the points improves the error only by √10 ≈ 3.2."}
],
"p14m6":[
 {id:"p14m6q1", q:"Request latency has a standard deviation of about 40 ms. With 100 samples the standard error of the mean is 4 ms. How many samples do you need for a standard error of 1 ms?", options:["400 samples, four times as many","40 samples, since 40 ms ÷ 1 ms = 40","1,600 samples, since SE = σ/√n","200 samples, since doubling n halves the error"], answer:2, why:"SE = σ/√n, so 1 = 40/√n gives √n = 40 and n = 1,600. Cutting the error by four needs sixteen times the data, the 1/√n law. Four times the samples (400) only halves it, to 2 ms."},
 {id:"p14m6q2", q:"A benchmark report gives a 95% confidence interval for mean page-load time of 1.8 s to 2.2 s. Which sentence interprets it correctly?", options:["About 95% of individual page loads take between 1.8 and 2.2 seconds","There is a 95% probability that the true mean lies somewhere inside this particular interval","About 95% of intervals built this way on repeated samples would cover the true mean","The mean is exactly 2.0 s, and the interval only reflects rounding"], answer:2, why:"The true mean is fixed; the interval is the random part, so 95% describes the method's coverage over repeated samples. The '95% probability' wording is the classic misreading, and the interval concerns the mean, not individual page loads, which spread far more widely."},
 {id:"p14m6q3", q:"Variant A converts 300 of 15,000 visitors (2.0%); B converts 360 of 15,000 (2.4%). Using the pooled two-proportion z-test, what do you conclude at α = 0.05?", options:["z ≈ 1.93, p ≈ 0.054: not significant, like any 0.4-point lift","z ≈ 2.36, p ≈ 0.02: significant at the 0.05 level","The 20% relative lift proves B is better without any test","z ≈ 0.4, since the difference is 0.4 percentage points"], answer:1, why:"Pooled p = 660/30,000 = 0.022; SE = √(0.022 × 0.978 × 2/15,000) ≈ 0.00169; z = 0.004/0.00169 ≈ 2.36, two-sided p ≈ 0.018. The same 0.4-point lift with 10,000 per arm gives z ≈ 1.93; larger samples shrink the standard error, so significance depends on n."},
 {id:"p14m6q4", q:"A team compares 10 page variants with the control. All are pure noise, and each is tested at α = 0.05. How likely is at least one 'significant' result, and what simple fix keeps that rate near 5%?", options:["5%, since each test has α = 0.05; no fix is needed","50%, since 10 × 0.05 = 0.5; test each at α = 0.5","About 40%, from 1 − 0.95^10; loosen each test's threshold to α = 0.5","About 40%, from 1 − 0.95^10; test each at α = 0.005 (Bonferroni)"], answer:3, why:"P(no false positive in 10 independent tests) = 0.95^10 ≈ 0.60, so at least one appears about 40% of the time. Bonferroni tests each at α/m = 0.05/10 = 0.005, keeping the family-wide rate near 5%. Adding 10 × 0.05 overcounts, because the events overlap."},
 {id:"p14m6q5", q:"Fit y = β₀ + β₁x by least squares to the points (1, 2), (2, 3) and (3, 7). What are the slope and the intercept?", options:["Slope 2.5, intercept −1","Slope 2.5, intercept 4","Slope 5, intercept −6","Slope 2, intercept 0"], answer:0, why:"With x̄ = 2 and ȳ = 4, Σ(x − x̄)(y − ȳ) = 2 + 0 + 3 = 5 and Σ(x − x̄)² = 2, so β₁ = 2.5 and β₀ = ȳ − β₁x̄ = −1. Slope 5 forgets to divide by Σ(x − x̄)²; intercept 4 is ȳ, not the line's value at x = 0."}
],
"p14m7":[
 {id:"p14m7q1", q:"Gradient descent on f(x) = 3x² starts at x = 10. What happens with learning rates η = 0.1, 0.3 and 0.4?", options:["All three converge, because f is convex","0.1 shrinks x by 0.4 per step, 0.3 alternates but converges, 0.4 diverges","0.1 diverges, and 0.4 converges fastest because its steps are largest","0.1 converges, while 0.3 and 0.4 both diverge because they overshoot"], answer:1, why:"f′(x) = 6x, so each step multiplies x by 1 − 6η: 0.4 at η = 0.1, −0.8 at η = 0.3 (overshoots, yet shrinks) and −1.4 at η = 0.4 (grows). Convexity guarantees a single minimum, not convergence; the step must keep |1 − 6η| below 1."},
 {id:"p14m7q2", q:"One neuron: z = wx + b, a = σ(z), loss L = (a − y)². With x = 2, y = 1 and a forward pass that gives a = 0.8, what is ∂L/∂w?", options:["−0.4, from 2(a − y)","−0.064, from 2(a − y) · a(1 − a), stopping at z","−0.8, from 2(a − y) · x","−0.128, from 2(a − y) · a(1 − a) · x"], answer:3, why:"The chain rule multiplies one local derivative per link: ∂L/∂a = 2(a − y) = −0.4, ∂a/∂z = a(1 − a) = 0.16 and ∂z/∂w = x = 2, giving −0.128. Each distractor drops a link; backpropagation is exactly this product, computed from the output backwards."},
 {id:"p14m7q3", q:"A log field takes four values with probabilities 1/2, 1/4, 1/8 and 1/8. What is the fewest bits per value that any lossless code can average?", options:["2 bits, since there are four possible values","1.75 bits, the entropy of the distribution","1 bit, the surprise of the most common value","1.5 bits, the average of 1 bit and 2 bits"], answer:1, why:"H = ½·1 + ¼·2 + ⅛·3 + ⅛·3 = 1.75 bits, and Shannon's source coding theorem makes that the floor; the Huffman code 0, 10, 110, 111 reaches it exactly. Two bits per value is what a fixed-length code spends, ignoring that the source is predictable."},
 {id:"p14m7q4", q:"A link has 10 MHz of bandwidth at S/N = 15. What is its Shannon capacity, and what does roughly doubling the transmit power, to S/N = 31, give?", options:["40 Mb/s, then 80 Mb/s, since capacity doubles with power","150 Mb/s, then 310 Mb/s, from B × S/N","40 Mb/s, then 50 Mb/s: about one more bit per symbol","160 Mb/s, then 320 Mb/s, from B × (1 + S/N)"], answer:2, why:"C = B log₂(1 + S/N): 10^7 × log₂ 16 = 40 Mb/s and 10^7 × log₂ 32 = 50 Mb/s. Capacity grows with the logarithm of power, so doubling it adds about one bit per symbol rather than doubling the rate; bandwidth is the term that scales linearly."},
 {id:"p14m7q5", q:"A 30 km single-mode span at 1310 nm (0.35 dB/km) has 6 fusion splices (0.1 dB each) and 2 connector pairs (0.5 dB each). The transmitter launches −3 dBm; the receiver needs −24 dBm. What are the total loss and the margin?", options:["Loss 7.6 dB, margin 13.4 dB","Loss 12.1 dB, margin 11.9 dB","Loss 10.5 dB, margin 10.5 dB","Loss 12.1 dB, margin 8.9 dB"], answer:3, why:"Fiber 30 × 0.35 = 10.5 dB, splices 0.6 dB and connectors 1.0 dB give 12.1 dB. The budget is −3 − (−24) = 21 dB, so the margin is 8.9 dB. 7.6 dB uses the 1550 nm figure of 0.2 dB/km, and 11.9 dB forgets that the transmitter starts at −3 dBm, not 0."}
]
});
